le": "Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Timbuktu, Mali", "role": "Major Cultural Center"}, {"name": "Cairo, Egypt", "role": "Source of Information"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Sandaki", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "sandaki"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7415950", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandaki_(mansa)"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Sandaki's brief and contested reign exemplifies the severe political instability that gripped the Mali Empire in the late 14th century, signaling a significant weakening of central authority and contributing to its eventual decline. His story, though minor in length, illustrates the broader historical forces at play during this transformative period for West Africa.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-12T03:55:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29177830473", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Sandaki, also known as Sandiki or Santigi, was the Mansa of the Mali Empire from 1388 to 1389. According to Ibn Khaldun Sandaki was not his name but rather his title, the Mandinka word for 'vizier'. His real name is unknown.", "newValue": "Sandaki, a pivotal figure in the late 14th-century Mali Empire, briefly held the reins of power as Mansa from 1388 to 1389, though his true name remains obscured by historical records. Known primarily by his title, derived from the Mandinka word for 'vizier' or 'chief minister', Sandaki's ascent marked a period of intense internal instability and factionalism within the once-dominant West African state. His short reign, documented by the Arab historian Ibn Khaldun, underscores the growing challenges to central authority and the fragmentation of the imperial succession system following the death of the powerful Mansa Musa I decades earlier.

Sandaki's brief tenure as Mansa was characterized by a struggle for control against Maghan II, the son of the previous ruler, Mansa Mari Djata II. After the death of Mansa Musa II in 1388, Sandaki, who had served as vizier, maneuvered himself onto the throne, effectively usurping the traditional line of succession. His rule was short-lived, however, as Maghan II successfully rallied support and, with the backing of key factions, deposed Sandaki in 1389. This internal power struggle, culminating in Sandaki's death in 1390, highlights the increasingly violent and contested nature of the Malian succession during this period of imperial decline.

The tumultuous reign of Sandaki, irrespective of his actual name, serves as a stark illustration of the Mali Empire's weakening political structure in the late 14th century. The ease with which a vizier could seize the throne, only to be overthrown by a rival claimant within a year, signaled a departure from the more stable, albeit sometimes contested, successions of earlier periods. This era of internal strife directly contributed to the decentralization of power and the eventual rise of successor states like the Songhai Empire, which would ultimately eclipse Mali's dominance in the region."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T03:55:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29177830473", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The decline of central authority within the Mali Empire following the death of Mansa Musa I created a power vacuum.", "Internal factionalism and disputes over imperial succession weakened the traditional mechanisms for transferring power.", "Sandaki's position as vizier provided him with significant administrative and political influence, enabling a power grab."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T03:55:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29177830473", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1390"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1390"}], "newValue": ["Sandaki's usurpation and subsequent overthrow further destabilized the Mali Empire's political landscape.", "The period of contested succession contributed to the decentralization of power away from the imperial capital.", "The i